    "uid": "3927d848937b4b60b0aca88fd41eaebc",
    "title": "Process memory read from /proc",
    "desc": "A program read another process's memory through /proc - dd/cat/cp over /proc/<pid>/mem or /proc/<pid>/maps, or gcore/gdb dumping a live process. Scraping a running process's address space is how credentials, keys and secrets are harvested straight from memory (MITRE T1003.007), bypassing files on disk entirely. A process reading /proc/<pid>/mem is almost never legitimate application behaviour, so it is a high-confidence signal.",
    "analytic": {
      "type_id": 1,
      "type": "Rule",
      "name": "proc_memory_dump",
